  "canonical_example": {
    ":db/id": 17592186068501,
    ":question/id": "e1662bab-c11d-5770-8b7d-6646902f7e20",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-1435",
    ":question/problem": "Solve the equation $x^2 + 2x = 6$.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "The first step is to put the equation into standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x &= 6 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2x - 6 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficients of our quadratic equation are\n\n$$\na = 1, \\quad b = 2, \\quad c = -6\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-(2) \\pm \\sqrt{(2)^2 - 4(1)(-6)}}{2(1)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{28}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 2\\sqrt{7}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= -1 \\pm \\sqrt{7} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = -1 + \\sqrt{7}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx = -1 - \\sqrt{7}\n$$"
  },
